Split Learning (SL) is a distributed learning framework that offers lightweight collaborative deep learning on resource-constrained devices. However, SL's distributed nature makes it vulnerable to client-side backdoor attacks. In this scenario, an adversary client can maliciously influence the global model on the server. The existing defenses heavily rely on the spatial consensus mechanism, which compares client updates and filters out statistical outliers. We demonstrate that such defenses do not hold in a non-IID data distribution environment, where honest clients naturally have gradients with high divergence. Thus, the state-of-the-art defenses suffer from a high false positive rate during the training rounds. This results in excluding benign participants and causing model instability at convergence. To this end, the proposed GradSent replaces spatial agreement with temporal consistency. The key idea is that although benign clients may differ from one another at each step, they tend to follow a stable and convergent trajectory when viewed across the training history. GradSent captures this behavior through two phase framework that first learns the manifold of benign dynamics with a lightweight Gradient Autoencoder (GAE) and then evaluates new updates against this learned temporal profile. It further employs iterative self-cleaning to remove possible contaminations from the burn-in history and dynamic thresholding to adapt to shrinking gradients. Our experiments on various datasets and architectures demonstrate that GradSent effectively blocks most of the client-side backdoor attacks while preserving Main Task Accuracy under extreme non-IID data distributions.